<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tahmasp II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1704">1704</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tahmasp II was a Safavid prince who claimed the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> after the Afghan capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1722 brought down his father's rule. The warlord Nader, then known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Qoli</a>, rose to prominence in his service and expelled Afghan, Ottoman and Russian occupiers in his name. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> describe how Nader later set Tahmasp aside and eventually proclaimed himself Shah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tahmasp-ii.jpg" alt="Tahmasp II" width="266" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mirza Mahdi Khan Astarabadi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadir_at_the_court_of_Shah_Tahmasp_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
